  "preamble": "Stopping of the voiced dental fricative [ð] to [d]. It happened historically in West Germanic (fricative [ð] > [d] in all positions), in North Germanic Swedish (Old Norse góðr > god), and in urban Egyptian and Levantine Arabic (ذ > d). It is also a well-known feature of many English dialects (th-stopping).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Stopping, Fortition",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "English dialects (New York City, Sheffield, Inland North)",
      "languageFamily": "West Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "the [ðə], than [ðæn]",
          "to": "[də], [dæn] (than = Dan)",
          "note": "Th-stopping; Sheffield speech is nicknamed 'dee-dar' for initial /ð/ > /d/."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Swedish",
      "languageFamily": "North Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Old Norse góðr /ˈɡoːðr̩/ 'good' (Old Swedish goþer)",
          "to": "god /ɡuːd/"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Egyptian Arabic",
      "languageFamily": "Semitic",
      "examples": [
